 "note": {
  "headline": "Week 5: ten lineups built around the chalk game, not inside it",
  "lede": "DET @ ARI (DET \u22125.5, 54.5) is the slate's top total by eight points, and the field knows it: five of the six most popular players on the slate (Gibbs, McBride, Michael Wilson, Amon-Ra, Brissett) play in it, and about three in ten field lineups carry three or more players from that game. If it shoots out, those lineups crowd the top of the standings, so ignoring it is a losing bet. Playing it the way everyone else does just makes you a duplicate. These ten take a different route into ARI/DET: Goff stacks (not Brissett), the low-owned side of each offense as the bring-back, and the chalk pieces used one at a time as a second-game pair. The rest of the set stacks the 45+ totals the field is skipping (LV\u2013NE, CHI\u2013GB, SF\u2013SEA).",
  "concepts": [
   {
    "k": "Stack the QB",
    "stat": "QB + 2 pass-catchers: 28.5% of the field \u2192 33.5% of top-1% lineups; QB + 2 WR + TE: 1.5% \u2192 7.4% of top-3",
    "body": "When a QB throws for 300 yards and three touchdowns, the points land on his receivers too. You collect them twice. Every lineup here pairs the QB with at least two of his own pass-catchers. A naked QB is what the field builds, and it under-performs in winning lineups."
   },
   {
    "k": "Bring it back",
    "stat": "Any bring-back 43% \u2192 51% of top-1%; an RB bring-back 10.5% \u2192 22.2% of top-3",
    "body": "A big game needs both teams scoring. One player from the QB's opponent turns the stack into a bet on the game going over, not on one team. The opponent's running back is the bring-back the field skips: he scores when his team is chasing points or salting the game away."
   },
   {
    "k": "Play a second game",
    "stat": "\u22651 opposing pair outside the QB game: 49.5% of the field \u2192 66.7% of top-3",
    "body": "The field scatters its other six players across six or seven games. Winners cluster: two players on opposite sides of a second game (RB vs WR is the best version), and never two teammates outside the QB stack, since they split one offense's points."
   },
   {
    "k": "Ownership is the edge",
    "stat": "Milly winners: ~113% total ownership, 4\u20135 players under 10%, ~2 over 20% (169 winners)",
    "body": "You don't win a big field by fading every popular player. You win it by owning the one or two chalk pieces that actually hit, and by being different everywhere else. Each player below carries an ownership tier. Every lineup keeps chalk to two pieces at most, and we estimate each one is unique in a 160,000-entry field."
   }
  ],
  "method": "Ranked by BirdieBuddy's copula sim: 20,000 simulated slates on nflverse role correlations, projections blended with sportsbook player props. Each lineup is scored against a 20,000-lineup field model calibrated to projected ownership. A lineup's top-1% rate is how often it beat 99% of that field; an average field lineup does it 1% of the time. Lineups were cross-checked in a second, independent field sim, and all ten finished in its top 15%. Ownership is shown as a tier only."
 },
